    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sprakers grew around a 1795 homestead built by pioneer George Spraker, a German immigrant who settled the fertile Mohawk River flats in 1755.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://fulton.nygenweb.net/Turnpike/Sprakers.html",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The hamlet\u2019s name honors the Spraker family, whose members fought in the Revolutionary War and later ran a tavern that served travelers on the Mohawk Turnpike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/sprakers_new_york",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sprakers station on the West Shore Railroad once housed a telegraph, express office, and post office, making it a key communication hub in the 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://fulton.nygenweb.net/Turnpike/Sprakers.html",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Mohawk River, which borders Sprakers to the north, has historically flooded the area, shaping local agriculture and prompting early flood\u2011control efforts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
